You are part of a controlled experiment comparing LLM coding agents.
Every agent receives this same prompt. You are <model>.
This prompt is also in PROMPT.md in your directory. Below, ROOT means <root>.

RULES
- Work only in ROOT/<run>/, your own git repository. Commit as you go; the last
  commit is graded. You have up to <budget> of wall-clock time, after which the run
  is stopped. When the work is complete, end your final message with the word DONE.
- Read-only inputs:
    ROOT/micm
        MICM source, commit f020eefb (v3.7.0-257)
    ROOT/micm/build, ROOT/micm/build-single
        MICM's tests built in double and in single precision (MICM_USE_DOUBLE=OFF);
        all pass. MICM runs them from the build directory, where some read and write
        files, so to run them, copy the build directory into your own directory.
    ROOT/reference
        test-case lists, TS1 inputs, MICM's TS1 results, and tools; see README.md
- Apart from these, do not read other directories under ROOT. Do not modify
  anything outside your directory, install software, or download code. Use no
  third-party code; libc, libm, pthreads, libdispatch, libomp, and macOS system
  frameworks are allowed.
- Do not put your model or vendor name in any file. Reviews are blind.
- No one will answer questions. When something is unspecified, decide, and record the
  decision and your reasoning in DECISIONS.md.

MACHINE AND TOOLCHAIN
- Apple M5 Pro laptop: 18 CPU cores (6 "Super" + 12 "Performance"), 20-core GPU
  (Metal 4), 24 GB unified memory, macOS 26.7.1. Command Line Tools only (no Xcode).
- Compiler: Apple clang 21.0.0 (/usr/bin/clang). Build: GNU Make 3.81 (/usr/bin/make).
- OpenMP: libomp 23.1.3 in ~/homebrew/opt/libomp. With Apple clang, compile with
  -Xpreprocessor -fopenmp -I$HOME/homebrew/opt/libomp/include and link with
  -L$HOME/homebrew/opt/libomp/lib -lomp.
- Also installed: cmake 4.4.4 in ~/homebrew/bin (MICM builds with it; your code builds
  with make) and Python 3.13 (/usr/local/bin/python3, for tooling only).
- GPU APIs: Metal and OpenCL 1.2. The GPU has no double precision: Metal rejects
  `double` and OpenCL lacks cl_khr_fp64. Compile Metal shaders at run time; there is
  no offline `metal` compiler.

TASK
Port MICM's CPU functionality to C and optimize it for performance on this machine.
The CUDA and Kokkos backends are out of scope.

Correctness is defined by MICM's test suite: the 676 cases in
ROOT/reference/micm_cases.txt, which are 669 GoogleTest cases and 7 tests that are
plain programs. Port every case, checking the same quantities with the same or
tighter tolerances, so that your library gives MICM's answers within numerical
roundoff. A plain-program case passes when your port of the program runs and prints
the same results. Where a case tests C++ mechanics, test the equivalent C behavior
(for example, an error code where MICM throws). A case with no meaningful C
equivalent may be marked N/A with a one-line reason. `make test-cpu` and
`make test-gpu` each print one line per case: `<id> PASS|FAIL|N/A <reason>`, with
<id> as in the case list.

Keep MICM's numerical methods: the solver formulas and parameter sets, error control,
tolerances, and LU algorithms. Everything else is your design: data layout, threading,
how cells are grouped and scheduled (including separate solver state per group of
cells, as MICM's OpenMP tutorial does), vectorization, memory management, and code
generation. Two limits:
- The library stays mechanism-generic. Code specialized to a mechanism is allowed only
  if your library generates it automatically, for any mechanism, and the tests
  exercise that path. No hand-written TS1-specific code.
- The benchmark runs through the same library code the tests exercise.

Write the library in C; you may also write parts of it directly in LLVM IR (.ll files
compiled by the same clang). GPU kernels may be Metal Shading Language or OpenCL C,
and Metal may be called through a thin Objective-C file that contains only API calls.
Python or shell may be used for one-time porting work and tooling, but building,
testing, and running the library must need only C and make. Design the solvers so
later experiments can replace or modify them.

Tune compiler flags; that is part of the task. Any MICM timing you compare against
must come from MICM built with the same compiler and the same settings (optimization
level, target CPU, floating-point options, LTO, PGO). `make micm-ref` builds
ROOT/reference/micm_ts1_ref.cpp with clang++ and your settings into
bin/micm_ts1_ref.

CONFIGURATIONS
1. `make cpu`  CPU only, double precision.
2. `make gpu`  CPU and GPU. Runs part of every TS1 time step on the GPU, and may use
               single precision. `make test-gpu` must pass the way MICM's
               single-precision build does: with the float tolerances in
               ROOT/micm/test/precision_matchers.hpp, skipping only the 24 cases that
               build skips (marked SKIPPED in ROOT/reference/micm_cases_single.txt).

TS1 BENCHMARK
TS1 is the mechanism in ROOT/micm/test/benchmark/ts1_mechanism.hpp (210 species, 547
reactions). `make bench` builds bin/ts1_bench_cpu and bin/ts1_bench_gpu:

    bin/ts1_bench_<config> [--threads N] CELLS STEPS DT CELLS_CSV

- Solver: MICM's three-stage Rosenbrock with its default parameters and tolerances,
  as in ROOT/micm/test/benchmark/micm_bench.cpp.
- CELLS_CSV: a header row naming temperature, pressure, and species or rate-parameter
  labels, then one row per cell; every cell differs. Columns not given take the
  defaults in TS1's InitState. InitState sets six photolysis rates (jo2_a, jh2o_b,
  jh2o_c, jch4_a, jch4_b, jhf) to 1 s-1, which is unphysical at its surface
  conditions; the input files set them to 0.
- Run one untimed warm-up step, then time STEPS steps of DT seconds. Print
  `elapsed_ms=<ms>` and the solver statistics summed over the timed steps as
  `internal_steps= accepted= rejected= function_calls= jacobian_updates=
  decompositions= solves=`. Write the final state, after the warm-up and the STEPS
  steps, to ts1_final.csv in the working directory: a header row of temperature,
  pressure, and species names, then one row per cell. ROOT/reference/micm_ts1_ref.cpp does
  exactly this with MICM.
- Use all cores by default; --threads N limits the run to N threads.

EVALUATION
- Gate: every non-N/A case passes in each configuration, and in every cell and
  species the TS1 final state y agrees with MICM's double-precision result y_MICM:
      make cpu:  |y - y_MICM| <= 1e-30 + 1e-10 |y_MICM|   (mol m-3)
      make gpu:  |y - y_MICM| <= 1e-30 + 2e-3  |y_MICM|
  For scale, MICM's own result moves by about 2e-14 relative under other compiler
  flags, the other LU algorithm, or another cell grouping, and by up to 1.6e-4 in
  single precision. ROOT/reference/compare_final.py applies these checks.
- Primary ranking: wall time of `make cpu` on 10,000 cells, 100 steps, DT = 30 s,
  with a CELLS_CSV made by ROOT/reference/make_cells_csv.py from an undisclosed seed. The
  experimenter times each entry on an idle machine (median of 5 runs); your own
  timings are not used. `make gpu` is ranked separately. For context, MICM built with
  -O3 -DNDEBUG takes <micm_ms> ms for this run on one core; the experimenter also
  times MICM built by your `make micm-ref`.
- Secondary ranking: blind review of readability, maintainability, and extensibility.
  Reviewers will try to add a Rosenbrock parameter set, replace the step-size
  controller, swap the linear solver, and add a rate-constant type.

DELIVERABLES
README.md (build, test, run), DESIGN.md (architecture, and how to make the changes
the reviewers will try), DECISIONS.md, RESULTS.md (test summary, and benchmark
results compared with MICM built with the same settings).
